Function source text that can be packed while nothing reads it.

Every function keeps its text for Function.prototype.toString, shared
per script (patches 0006 and 0008). Nothing else reads it, yet on
Reddit it was 4.4 MB of the page's script heap, on the host and on the
PowerPC alike.

The embedder now gives the runtime a pair of hooks
(JS_SetSourcePackHooks): one packs a text into a block from
js_malloc_rt, the other unpacks it into a buffer of the text's length.
JS_PackSourceTexts packs each text of a minimum length that was not
read since the previous call; a text read in between waits for the next
call. A JSSourceText's buf is therefore a block of its own, NULL while
the text is packed, and js_source_text_buf unpacks it on demand (toString,
the bytecode writer with source, the lookup of a whole script's text by
its content). The packed copy stays beside an unpacked buf, so packing
the same text again only frees buf.

A function's text was a pointer into the shared buf; it is now an
offset (JSFunctionBytecode.source_ofs), so the buf can go and come back.

JS_ComputeMemoryUsage counts a text's header, its unpacked bytes when
present and its packed bytes.

Aura packs with raw deflate, level 1, texts of 4 KB or more, every 5
seconds of a runtime's clock when script is left (js_code_expire). On
Reddit the text fell from 4.4 MB to 1.6 MB.

